From: CoreRouter <noreply@corerouter>
Subject: [PATCH] ospfd: don't assert on a missing router-LSA in the TI-LFA
 reverse SPF

G3.4l (docs/frr-tilfa-abort.md). With "fast-reroute ti-lfa", the Q spaces
are reverse SPF trees. ospf_spf_next() reads the reverse distance of a
point-to-point link from W's router-LSA before it checks that the LSA is
in the LSDB (RFC 2328 16.1 (2b), a few lines below), so a router-LSA of V
that names a neighbor W whose own router-LSA is not there (not received
yet while adjacencies come up, or already flushed) hits
assert(w_lsa && w_lsa->data) in get_reverse_distance() and ospfd aborts.
The forward SPF just skips W in that case; the reverse one now does the
same. Reproduced in FRR 10.7.1 and master (tests/ospfd topo6, and in the
lab VM); the backup paths of the other prefixes do not change. To be
dropped once upstream FRR carries the fix.

---
--- a/ospfd/ospf_spf.c
+++ b/ospfd/ospf_spf.c
@@ -1405,9 +1405,14 @@ static void ospf_spf_next(struct vertex *v, struct ospf_area *area,
 			/*
 			 * For TI-LFA we might need the reverse SPF.
 			 * Currently only works with P2P!
+			 *
+			 * W's router-LSA may not be in the LSDB (not received
+			 * yet, or already flushed) while V still advertises
+			 * the link: then there is no reverse distance to read,
+			 * and the check below skips W, as the forward SPF does.
 			 */
 			if (type == LSA_LINK_TYPE_POINTOPOINT
-			    && area->spf_reversed)
+			    && area->spf_reversed && w_lsa)
 				link_distance =
 					get_reverse_distance(v, l, w_lsa);
 			else
